@* The one place alert markup is typed. Parameters, the dismissed-state model
   and every rule below are documented on the code-behind (Notice.razor.cs).

   Attribute order is load-bearing: the caller's attributes are splatted
   first, so the component's own class (which already contains the caller's,
   merged) is the one that lands. The names a caller's attribute would
   contradict or silently lose to — role, onclick and the live-region
   attributes — are refused in OnParametersSet rather than overridden here.

   The live-region role sits on the content wrapper, not on the box. A status
   or alert region is implicitly atomic, so everything inside it is presented
   with the message; the close button is the box's control, not part of what
   the notice says, and as the wrapper's sibling it is outside the region. A
   notice announced by an enclosing region has a null role, and the renderer
   omits an attribute whose value is null.

   One click handler, on the box. The close button deliberately carries none:
   its click bubbles to the box, so both gestures take the same single path
   and neither can fire twice. A non-dismissible notice's BoxClick has no
   delegate, and the renderer elides an event attribute whose callback has
   none, so such a box carries no handler at all.

   The content wrapper is also what keeps inner interactive content from
   dismissing: a click that originates inside it never reaches the box.
   Notice.razor.css hands clicks on the wrapper's non-interactive content
   back to the box, which is how the whole box stays the large target. *@
@if (!IsDismissed)
{
    <div @attributes="AdditionalAttributes" class="@BoxClass" @onclick="BoxClick">
        <div class="bg-notice-content" role="@ContentRole" @onclick:stopPropagation="Dismissible">@ChildContent</div>
        @if (Dismissible)
        {
            <button type="button" class="btn-close" aria-label="@CloseButtonLabel"></button>
        }
    </div>
}
